  <info>
   <title>Configuring the network</title>
    <meta name="maintainer" content="cwickert@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
    <abstract><!-- can be changed via merge in the assembly -->
      <para>
       Manage network connections in the <guilabel>Network</guilabel> screen.
      </para>
    </abstract>
  </info>
  <figure xml:id="fig-deployment-manual-network">
    <title>&agama; installer network settings</title>
    <mediaobject>
      <textobject role="description">
        <phrase>Screenshot of the &agama; installer for &productname; &productnumber; showing the
          network configuration page. It lists active network interfaces, IP addresses, and options
          to add new wired or Wi-Fi connections.
        </phrase>
      </textobject>
      <imageobject role="fo">
        <imagedata fileref="agama-installation-sles-network.png" width="100%"/>
      </imageobject>
      <imageobject role="html">
        <imagedata fileref="agama-installation-sles-network.png" width="100%"/>
      </imageobject>
    </mediaobject>
  </figure>
  <para>
    The overview shows the configured network connections in a table. Click the headers to sort the
    connections by name, type, state, device, or IP address. You can also filter by name (with
    wildcards), type and state.
  </para>
  <para>
  </para>
  <section xml:id="reference-deployment-manual-network-edit">
    <title>Editing a connection</title>
    <para>
      By default, &agama; configures a connection with DHCP. To edit a connection, click the three dots at the
      end or the line. The following options are available:
    </para>
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Details</term>
        <listitem>
          <para>Shows the connection details, both the current status and the configuration.
          </para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Edit connection</term>
        <listitem>
          <para>
            Edits the connection. For details, refer to <xref linkend="reference-deployment-manual-network-add"/>.
          </para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Disconnect</term>
        <listitem>
          <para>
            Temporarily disconnects the connection.
          </para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Delete</term>
        <listitem>
          <para>
            Deletes the connection and frees the device for a different connection.
          </para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="reference-deployment-manual-network-add" >
    <title>Adding a new connection</title>
    <para>
      Click <guimenu>Add connection</guimenu> to configure a new network connection. The following
      types are supported:
    </para>
    <variablelist role="tabs">
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Ethernet</term>
        <listitem>
          <variablelist>
            <title>Ethernet settings</title>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Name</term>
              <listitem>
                <para>
                  Name of the connection. Meaningful names can use the destination
                  (<replaceable>Internet</replaceable>), the connection type
                  (<replaceable>Ethernet</replaceable>) or the device
                  (<replaceable>eth0</replaceable>, if device binding is enabled).
                  Free text, but for practical reasons it is recommended to avoid special characters.
                </para>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Device Binding</term>
              <listitem>
                <variablelist>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>None</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        The connection is available for all devices. After a reboot or relocatation
                        the system, the device may change.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Chosen by name</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        The connection is identified by its name in the system.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Chosen by MAC</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        The connection is identified by its physical address.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                </variablelist>
                <para>
                  One of <guilabel>None</guilabel>
                </para>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>IPv4 settings</term>
              <listitem>
                <variablelist>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Automatic</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        IP address and gateway are assigned from the network with DHCP.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Manual</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Static IP address, netmask and gateway. Enter an IPv4 address such as
                        <replaceable>192.168.1.1.</replaceable> or
                        <replaceable>192.168.1.1/24</replaceable> The prefix is added automatically if
                        omitted.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Automatic + Manual</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        DHCP configuration from the network plus static addresses and gateway.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                </variablelist>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>IPv6 settings</term>
              <listitem>
                <variablelist>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Automatic</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        IP address and gateway are assigned from the network with DHCP.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Manual</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Static IP address, netmask and gateway. Enter an IPv6 address such as
                        <replaceable>2001:db8::1</replaceable> or
                        <replaceable>2001:db8::1/64</replaceable>. The prefix is added automatically if
                        omitted.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Automatic + Manual</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        DHCP configuration from the network plus static addresses and gateway.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                </variablelist>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Use custom DNS servers</term>
              <listitem>
                <para>
                  Enable and specify the addresses of the DNS servers.
                </para>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Use custom DNS search domains</term>
              <listitem>
                <para>
                  Enable and specify the DNS search domain such as
                  <replaceable>&exampledomain;</replaceable>.
                </para>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
          </variablelist>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Bonding</term>
        <listitem>
          <variablelist>
            <title>Bonding settings</title>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Name</term>
              <listitem>
                <para>
                  Name of the connection. Meaningful names can use the destination
                  (<replaceable>Internet</replaceable>), the connection type
                  (<replaceable>Bond</replaceable>) or the device
                  (<replaceable>bond0</replaceable>, if device binding is enabled).
                  Free text, but for practical reasons it is recommended to avoid special characters.
                </para>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Device name</term>
              <listitem>
                <para>
                  Provide a device name such as <replaceable>bond0</replaceable>.
                </para>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Bond mode</term>
              <listitem>
                <para>
                  Select the bonding mode:
                </para>
                <variablelist>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>balance-rr</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Packets are transmitted in round-robin fashion from the first to the last
                        available interface. Provides fault tolerance and load balancing. Requires
                        switch support. Certain switches might fail with this mode.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>active backup</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Only one network interface is active. If it fails, a different interface becomes
                        active. Provides fault tolerance. No specific switch support is required.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>balance-xor</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Traffic is split between all available interfaces based on the number of
                        interfaces included in the bonded device. Provides fault tolerance and load
                        balancing. Requires switch support. Certain switches might fail with this mode.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>broadcast</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        All traffic is broadcast on all interfaces. Provides fault tolerance.
                        Requires switch support. Certain switches might fail with this mode.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>802.3ad</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Also called LACP. All interfaces in the LACP group must share the same speed
                        and duplex settings, and must be connected to the same switch. Provides
                        fault tolerance and load balancing.
                        Requires <command>ethtool</command> support in the interface drivers, and a
                        switch that supports and is configured for IEEE 802.3ad Dynamic link
                        aggregation. If your switch supports it, this is the preferred mode.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>balance-tlb</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Adaptive transmit load balancing. Provides fault tolerance and load
                        balancing. Requires <command>ethtool</command> support in the interface
                        drivers. No specific switch support is required, but certain switches
                        might fail with this mode.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>balance-alb</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Adaptive load balancing. Provides fault tolerance and load balancing.
                        Requires <command>ethtool</command> support in the interface drivers. No
                        specific switch support is required, but certain switches might fail with
                        this mode.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                </variablelist>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>IPv4 settings</term>
              <listitem>
                <variablelist>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Automatic</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        IP address and gateway are assigned from the network with DHCP.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Manual</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Static IP address, netmask and gateway. Enter an IPv4 address such as
                        <replaceable>192.168.1.1.</replaceable> or
                        <replaceable>192.168.1.1/24</replaceable> The prefix is added automatically if
                        omitted.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Automatic + Manual</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        DHCP configuration from the network plus static addresses and gateway.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                </variablelist>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>IPv6 settings</term>
              <listitem>
                <variablelist>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Automatic</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        IP address and gateway are assigned from the network with DHCP.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Manual</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Static IP address, netmask and gateway. Enter an IPv6 address such as
                        <replaceable>2001:db8::1</replaceable> or
                        <replaceable>2001:db8::1/64</replaceable>. The prefix is added automatically if
                        omitted.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Automatic + Manual</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        DHCP configuration from the network plus static addresses and gateway.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                </variablelist>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
          </variablelist>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Bridge</term>
        <listitem>
          <variablelist>
            <title>Bridge settings</title>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Name</term>
              <listitem>
                <para>
                  Name of the connection. Meaningful names can use the destination
                  (<replaceable>Internet</replaceable>), the connection type
                  (<replaceable>Bridge</replaceable>) or the device
                  (<replaceable>br0</replaceable>, if device binding is enabled).
                  Free text, but for practical reasons it is recommended to avoid special characters.
                </para>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Device name</term>
              <listitem>
                <para>
                  Name of the device, such as <replaceable>br0</replaceable>
                </para>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>Spanning Tree Protocol (STP)</term>
              <listitem>
                <variablelist>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Default</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        (Enabled with the system default settings)
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Custom</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        Custom (Explicitly enabled with manual settings): Forward delay (optional)
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Disabled</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        (not used by this bridge)
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                </variablelist>
              </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
              <term>IPv4 settings</term>
              <listitem>
                <variablelist>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Automatic</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
                        IP address and gateway are assigned from the network with DHCP.
                      </para>
                    </listitem>
                  </varlistentry>
                  <varlistentry>
                    <term>Manual</term>
                    <listitem>
                      <para>
